      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; equation (1.1) and following paragraph",
      "anchor": "q : R × R → R^m; A is a constant m × m real matrix",
      "observation": "The introductory system is q_t(x,t) + A q_x(x,t) = 0. Its unknown has real spatial and temporal arguments and m real components. Subscripts denote partial derivatives."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; scalar specialization paragraph",
      "anchor": "m = 1 and the matrix A reduces to a scalar value",
      "observation": "The simplest case is a constant-coefficient scalar equation. The source says it is hyperbolic provided the scalar A is real."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; contaminant transport paragraph and equation (1.2)",
      "anchor": "one-dimensional pipe at constant velocity ū",
      "observation": "For a contaminant transported with the fluid, the concentration or density q(x,t) satisfies q_t(x,t) + ū q_x(x,t) = 0."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; equation (1.3) and following paragraph",
      "anchor": "q(x,t) = q̃(x − ūt); for any function q̃(ξ)",
      "observation": "The source asserts that translated profiles give solutions and describes their propagation with constant speed ū and unchanged shape."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26; printed page 4; section 1.1.1, equation (1.10)",
      "anchor": "total mass; flux function is f(q) = ūq",
      "observation": "The contaminant advection equation is attributed to conservation of contaminant mass and linear flux. Chemical reactions that change total mass require source terms."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF pages 26–27; printed pages 4–5; section 1.1.2",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth; does not hold in the classical sense",
      "observation": "Deriving the differential equation from integral conservation requires sufficient smoothness. At discontinuities the classical PDE does not hold, while the integral conservation law remains fundamental."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 37; printed page 15; Chapter 2 opening and equation (2.1)",
      "anchor": "chemical tracer; density; total mass of the tracer",
      "observation": "The explanatory model treats q as tracer density in a one-dimensional pipe. Integrating q over a pipe segment gives tracer mass. The one-dimensional density can be measured as mass per unit length."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 38; printed page 16; equations (2.2)–(2.6)",
      "anchor": "flux at (x,t) = u(x,t)q(x,t); u(x,t) = ū is some constant",
      "observation": "Transport flux is velocity times density. Constant velocity independent of both space and time gives f(q) = ūq. The integral balance is the left-end flux minus the right-end flux."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 39; printed page 17; derivation preceding section 2.1 and equation (2.12)",
      "anchor": "sufficiently smooth; scalar, linear, constant-coefficient PDE of hyperbolic type",
      "observation": "The explanatory derivation explicitly assumes enough smoothness for its calculus manipulations and obtains the same advection equation from the constant-velocity flux."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF pages 39–40; printed pages 17–18; equation (2.13) and surrounding discussion",
      "anchor": "Any smooth function of the form; x − ūt = constant",
      "observation": "The explanatory discussion qualifies translated-profile solutions as smooth and states that q is constant on characteristics x − ūt = constant."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 40; printed page 18; initial and boundary condition discussion",
      "anchor": "infinitely long pipe with no boundaries; If the pipe has finite length, a < x < b",
      "observation": "The source separately discusses an infinite spatial domain with initial data and a finite pipe requiring inflow boundary data. For ū > 0, its finite-pipe example places the boundary condition at the left endpoint."
    }
  ],
